\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Background}
The discovery of therapeutic compounds increasingly depends on computational models that predict molecular properties with high accuracy and efficiency \cite{schneider2018}.  Molecular property prediction—estimating physicochemical, biological, or pharmacokinetic attributes from a molecular graph—has therefore become a central task in cheminformatics and drug design.  Early methods represented molecules by fixed‑length fingerprints or descriptors and applied conventional machine‑learning algorithms such as random forests or support vector machines \cite{rogers2010}.  Recent advances employ graph‑based deep learning, where atoms are treated as nodes and chemical bonds as edges.  These approaches fall into three major families: (i) graph convolutional networks (GCNs) that aggregate neighbor information via spectral or spatial convolutions \cite{kipf2017}; (ii) message‑passing neural networks (MPNNs) that learn edge‑wise update functions to propagate information across the graph \cite{gilmer2017}; and (iii) attention‑based graph networks that assign learnable weights to different neighbors or substructures \cite{velivckovic2018}.  Although each family offers distinct advantages—GCNs are computationally efficient, MPNNs provide expressive message functions, and attention mechanisms enhance interpretability—their performance often plateaus on large, heterogeneous chemical datasets.

\subsection{Motivation}
Despite steady progress, several limitations persist.  First, many models exhibit poor generalization to out‑of‑distribution molecules, especially when training data are scarce or biased toward specific scaffolds \cite{zhang2020}.  Second, the learned representations are frequently opaque to medicinal chemists, who require clear rationales for predicted activities; for instance, standard MPNNs treat all bonds uniformly, obscuring the contributions of functional groups that drive bioactivity.  Third, most approaches rely solely on supervised learning and ignore the abundant unlabeled molecular graphs that could be exploited through self‑supervision.  These gaps motivate three research questions: (1) How can a graph‑based model be made robust to scaffold‑level distribution shifts? (2) In what ways can the model’s predictions be rendered chemically interpretable? (3) Can self‑supervised pretraining substantially improve downstream property prediction under limited labeled data?

\subsection{Proposed Approach}
To address the identified challenges, we introduce a hierarchical attention‑guided graph neural network (HA‑GNN) combined with a dual‑stage self‑supervised pretraining scheme.  HA‑GNN incorporates a multi‑scale attention mechanism that first aggregates atom‑level messages within local substructures (e.g., rings or functional groups) and then integrates these substructure embeddings at a higher, graph‑level hierarchy.  This hierarchical design captures fine‑grained chemical interactions and global molecular context, thereby enhancing robustness to scaffold variations.  The pretraining stage consists of two complementary tasks: (i) a motif‑reconstruction objective that forces the network to recover masked functional groups, and (ii) a graph‑level contrastive objective that discriminates between augmented views of the same molecule.  By jointly optimizing these self‑supervised signals, HA‑GNN learns rich, generalizable representations that can be fine‑tuned on downstream property prediction with few labeled examples.  The framework directly tackles the limitations of existing GCN/MPNN models—improving distributional robustness, providing chemically meaningful attention maps, and leveraging abundant unlabeled data.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{itemize}
    \item We propose a novel hierarchical attention architecture that simultaneously captures local substructure chemistry and global molecular topology, leading to improved predictive robustness across diverse scaffolds.
    \item We develop a dual‑stage self‑supervised pretraining paradigm—motif reconstruction and contrastive learning—that exploits large unlabeled molecular libraries to enrich feature learning.
    \item We demonstrate that HA‑GNN attains state‑of‑the‑art performance on multiple benchmark datasets, particularly under low‑resource conditions, confirming its practical utility.
    \item We provide extensive interpretability analyses showing that the learned attention weights align with known pharmacophores and functional groups, facilitating expert‑driven validation of model predictions.
\end{itemize}